A Sudoku solving algorithm can be designed using backtracking, which is an efficient way to find all possible solutions for the puzzle. The algorithm starts by filling in the empty cells with numbers from 1-9 that satisfy the constraints of each row, column and subgrid. If no valid number exists then it will backtrack and try another option until a solution is found or all possibilities have been exhausted. This process continues until the entire board has been filled out correctly. In addition, there are several heuristics such as checking if any rows/columns/subgrids already contain duplicate values before attempting further calculations - these help speed up computation time while still ensuring accuracy. Finally, once solved the algorithm outputs both the final result along with steps taken towards reaching this point so users understand how they arrived at their answer! 
